.TH passlit 1 "" "passlit 0.0.1"
.SH NAME
passlit \- utility to create passwords and codes that feel good to read,
type and print.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B passlit
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Write one sample per line to standard output.
Sample uniformly from all strings satisfying the selected constraints,
using exact integer counts and secure randomness.
Sampling is with replacement: duplicates are possible.
.PP
Defaults: one sample, alphabet U*L*D*O*, 128 bits rounded down.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-generate
Generate samples (default).
.TP
.B \-e, \-\-encode
Read whitespace-separated zero-based ranks from standard input; write
one sample per line.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-rank
Read whitespace-separated samples from standard input; write their
zero-based ranks, one per line.
Invalid samples produce -1. Invalid ranks produce an empty output line.
Both modes report invalid inputs on standard error, continue, and exit 65.
The alphabet, selected length and pattern policy must match in both directions.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-test
Validate the configuration without adjusting its length. Print the selected
length, exact scope size and entropy_bits: floor and ceiling of log2(scope).
No randomness is consumed.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-decimal
Use decimal ranks (default), with encode or rank.
.TP
.B \-x, \-\-hex
Use lowercase hexadecimal ranks. Output has a 0x prefix and enough leading
zeros to fit the largest valid rank. Input accepts either case for digits,
with an optional 0x, 0X, x or X prefix.
.TP
.BI "\-a, \-\-alphabet " SET
Select character groups; see ALPHABET.
.TP
.BI "\-c, \-\-custom " POOL
Supply characters for c or c*; does not enable the group by itself.
At most 256 non-whitespace bytes.
Duplicates and overlap with enabled built-in groups are removed.
.TP
.BI "\-l, \-\-length " N
Set a positive sample length. Cannot be combined with \-b or \-R.
In generate mode, if too short for required groups, use the minimum instead,
warn on standard error, and exit with status 65 after writing the batch.
Maximum: system page size clamped to 4096\-65536.
.TP
.BI "\-b, \-\-bits " N
Set a nonnegative bit target (default 128); see ROUNDING.
.TP
.BI "\-R, \-\-round " MODE
Use down or 0 (default), or up or 1 for bit targets.
.TP
.BI "\-n, \-\-number " N
Write N samples in generate mode (default: 1).
.TP
.BI "\-j, \-\-jobs " N
Use N workers (default: 0, automatic). Zero selects half the online CPUs,
with a minimum of one; builds without threads use one. Explicit N above
one requires a build with thread support.
Seeded samples and their order match sequential output.
Batches smaller than 256 samples run sequentially.
Encode and rank process stdin in bounded batches using the same workers.
Output and error reports retain input order.
.TP
.B \-P, \-\-reject-patterns
Enable local pattern rules (default): at most three identical characters
or four characters from one group consecutively, and no adjacent repeated
bigram (abab) or trigraph (abcabc).
The group limit applies only when multiple groups are available.
Singleton alphabets bypass the rules; the CLI warns and returns 65 if needed.
Counts and bit targets include the enabled rules.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-allow-patterns
Disable local pattern rules. Seeded output depends on the policy;
\-p retains the previous sequences.
.TP
.BI "\-s, \-\-seed " TEXT
Generate reproducible samples instead of using secure randomness.
The same seed and configuration reproduce the batch for this algorithm
version. Sample indices start at zero; longer batches retain their prefix.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show version.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show usage.
.SH ALPHABET
Concatenate selectors without spaces. Uppercase selectors choose standard
pools; lowercase selectors choose full ASCII groups, except c (custom).
.TP
.B U / u
ACEFHKLMNPRTVXYZ / A\-Z
.TP
.B L / l
abcdefghkmnpqstvxyz / a\-z
.TP
.B D / d
34679 / 0\-9
.TP
.B O / o
_\-.^+=?* / all ASCII punctuation
.TP
.B c
Custom pool supplied with \-c.
.PP
Omitted groups are excluded. Each group may appear only once;
standard and full variants are mutually exclusive.
Append * to require at least one character from that group.
For c*, this means a character remaining after overlap removal.
An empty required pool is impossible.
.PP
Quote selectors and pools to prevent shell expansion.
For example, "U*D" requires uppercase letters but leaves digits optional.
.SH ROUNDING
For the number S of valid strings at a given length, compare S with
2**N exactly. Down chooses the longest length with S <= 2**N;
up chooses the shortest length with S >= 2**N.
An unattainable target or length is an error.
.PP
A singleton alphabet supports explicit lengths and zero bits rounded up.
Positive bits rounded up are impossible; downward rounding has no finite
longest length.
.SH SEEDS
Predictable seeds give predictable samples. Bit targets describe the sample
space, not seed strength. Command-line seeds may appear in shell history
or process arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
passlit
passlit \-a "U*L*D*O*" \-l 22 \-n 1000
passlit \-a "D*c*" \-c "xyz" \-b 128 \-R up
passlit \-\-seed "example" \-n 10
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid arguments or configuration.
.TP
.B 65
Length increased, singleton rules relaxed, impossible constraints,
or no supported length fits.
.TP
.B 71
Allocation failure. GMP allocation failures abort instead.
.TP
.B 74
Random-source or output failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR passlit (3)
